<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Facade Pattern" id="facade">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1200">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="FACADE DESIGN PATTERN - Simplified Interface to Complex Subsystem" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- DEFINITION -->
        <mxCell id="def_title" value="PURPOSE: Hide system complexity from client by providing simplified interface" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- REAL-WORLD ANALOGY -->
        <mxCell id="analogy_title" value="REAL-WORLD ANALOGY: CAR" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="120" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="analogy" value="WITHOUT FACADE (Driver knows everything):&#xa;  Driver must understand:&#xa;    - Engine combustion process&#xa;    - Gear synchronization mechanism&#xa;    - Fuel injection system&#xa;    - Brake hydraulics&#xa;    - 1000+ components interaction&#xa;  &#xa;  To accelerate: Manually control engine, adjust gears, manage fuel...&#xa;  ✗ Too complex! Driver overwhelmed!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WITH FACADE (Simple interface):&#xa;  Driver only knows:&#xa;    - Press ACCELERATOR → Speed increases&#xa;    - Press BRAKE → Speed decreases&#xa;    - Turn STEERING → Car turns&#xa;  &#xa;  Facade hides complexity:&#xa;    - accelerate() internally manages engine, gears, fuel, etc.&#xa;    - brake() internally manages hydraulics, ABS, etc.&#xa;  &#xa;  ✓ Simple! Driver uses without knowing internals!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY INSIGHT:&#xa;  'Client doesn't care HOW speed increases, only THAT it increases'&#xa;  Facade provides simple methods, hides complex subsystem!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="160" width="1520" height="380" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <mxCell id="problem_title" value="THE PROBLEM: Client Exposed to Complex Subsystem" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="580" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="problem" value="EXAMPLE: Order Creation System&#xa;&#xa;SUBSYSTEM (complex!):&#xa;  class ProductDao { Product getProduct(id); }&#xa;  class Payment { boolean makePayment(amount); }&#xa;  class Invoice { void generateInvoice(order); }&#xa;  class Notification { void sendNotification(user); }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;CLIENT CODE (WITHOUT FACADE):&#xa;  class Client {&#xa;    void createOrder() {&#xa;      // Step 1: Get product&#xa;      ProductDao productDao = new ProductDao();&#xa;      Product product = productDao.getProduct(123);&#xa;      &#xa;      // Step 2: Make payment&#xa;      Payment payment = new Payment();&#xa;      boolean paid = payment.makePayment(product.getPrice());&#xa;      &#xa;      // Step 3: Generate invoice&#xa;      if (paid) {&#xa;        Invoice invoice = new Invoice();&#xa;        invoice.generateInvoice(product);&#xa;      }&#xa;      &#xa;      // Step 4: Send notification&#xa;      Notification notif = new Notification();&#xa;      notif.sendNotification(user);&#xa;    }&#xa;  }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;PROBLEMS:&#xa;  ✗ Client knows EVERY detail of order creation&#xa;  ✗ Client creates ALL objects (ProductDao, Payment, Invoice, Notification)&#xa;  ✗ Client knows the EXACT SEQUENCE (get → pay → invoice → notify)&#xa;  ✗ If sequence changes → Client must change!&#xa;  ✗ If Payment API changes (boolean → void) → Client breaks!&#xa;  ✗ If new step added (e.g., UpdateInventory) → Client must update!&#xa;  ✗ Code scattered, hard to maintain&#xa;  ✗ Tight coupling (client knows implementation details)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;IMAGINE:&#xa;  100 clients calling order creation&#xa;  New step added → 100 clients must change!&#xa;  Payment API changes → 100 clients break!&#xa;  DISASTER!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="620" width="740" height="720" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- SOLUTION -->
        <mxCell id="solution_title" value="THE SOLUTION: Facade Pattern" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="580" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="solution" value="FACADE LAYER:&#xa;&#xa;class OrderFacade {&#xa;  // Facade owns subsystem objects&#xa;  private ProductDao productDao;&#xa;  private Payment payment;&#xa;  private Invoice invoice;&#xa;  private Notification notification;&#xa;  &#xa;  public OrderFacade() {&#xa;    // Facade creates &amp; manages subsystem objects&#xa;    this.productDao = new ProductDao();&#xa;    this.payment = new Payment();&#xa;    this.invoice = new Invoice();&#xa;    this.notification = new Notification();&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  // SIMPLE INTERFACE for client!&#xa;  public void createOrder(int productId, User user) {&#xa;    // Step 1: Get product&#xa;    Product product = productDao.getProduct(productId);&#xa;    &#xa;    // Step 2: Make payment&#xa;    payment.makePayment(product.getPrice());&#xa;    &#xa;    // Step 3: Generate invoice&#xa;    invoice.generateInvoice(product);&#xa;    &#xa;    // Step 4: Send notification&#xa;    notification.sendNotification(user);&#xa;    &#xa;    System.out.println('Order created successfully!');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;CLIENT CODE (WITH FACADE):&#xa;  class Client {&#xa;    void main() {&#xa;      OrderFacade facade = new OrderFacade();&#xa;      facade.createOrder(123, currentUser);  // That's it!&#xa;    }&#xa;  }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;BENEFITS:&#xa;  ✓ Client only knows facade.createOrder()&#xa;  ✓ Client doesn't know: ProductDao, Payment, Invoice, Notification&#xa;  ✓ Client doesn't know sequence (get → pay → invoice → notify)&#xa;  ✓ New step added? Only facade changes (client unchanged!)&#xa;  ✓ Payment API changes? Only facade adapts (client unaffected!)&#xa;  ✓ Loose coupling (client knows facade only)&#xa;  ✓ Centralized logic (1 place to maintain)&#xa;  ✓ Easy testing (mock facade)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;CHANGE IMPACT:&#xa;&#xa;WITHOUT FACADE:&#xa;  New step added → 100 clients change ✗&#xa;  API change → 100 clients break ✗&#xa;&#xa;WITH FACADE:&#xa;  New step added → ONLY facade changes, 0 clients change ✓&#xa;  API change → ONLY facade adapts, 0 clients break ✓&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY PRINCIPLE:&#xa;  'Facade takes responsibility of managing subsystem complexity'&#xa;  'Client delegates to facade, trusts it handles details'&#xa;  'Like pressing car accelerator — don't care about engine internals!'" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="620" width="740" height="920" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- FACADE vs PROXY vs ADAPTER -->
        <mxCell id="vs_title" value="FACADE vs PROXY vs ADAPTER (Pattern Confusion!)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1380" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="vs_table" value="┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐&#xa;│  ASPECT      │  FACADE                       │  PROXY                        │  ADAPTER                      │&#xa;├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤&#xa;│  PURPOSE     │  SIMPLIFY complex subsystem   │  CONTROL ACCESS to ONE object │  CONVERT incompatible         │&#xa;│              │  (hide complexity)            │  (security, lazy load, cache) │  interfaces (make compatible) │&#xa;│              │                               │                               │                               │&#xa;│  SCOPE       │  MULTIPLE classes/subsystems  │  SINGLE object                │  TWO interfaces               │&#xa;│              │  (orchestrates many)          │  (wraps one)                  │  (bridges two)                │&#xa;│              │                               │                               │                               │&#xa;│  INTERFACE   │  NEW simplified interface     │  SAME interface as real       │  NEW interface (expected)     │&#xa;│              │  (facade.createOrder)         │  object (EmployeeDao)         │  different from existing      │&#xa;│              │                               │                               │                               │&#xa;│  CLIENT      │  Optional! Client CAN bypass  │  Transparent! Client doesn't  │  Required! Client needs       │&#xa;│  AWARE?      │  facade &amp; use subsystems      │  know proxy exists            │  adapter to use existing      │&#xa;│              │  directly                     │                               │                               │&#xa;│              │                               │                               │                               │&#xa;│  EXAMPLE     │  OrderFacade wraps:           │  EmployeeDaoProxy wraps       │  PowerAdapter:                │&#xa;│              │  - ProductDao                 │  EmployeeDao (one object)     │  Round plug → Square socket   │&#xa;│              │  - Payment                    │  Adds access control          │  Makes incompatible work      │&#xa;│              │  - Invoice                    │                               │                               │&#xa;│              │  - Notification               │                               │                               │&#xa;│              │  createOrder() orchestrates!  │                               │                               │&#xa;└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;SIMPLE RULES:&#xa;  Multiple subsystems, want simple interface?         → FACADE&#xa;  One object, want access control/caching?             → PROXY&#xa;  Two incompatible interfaces, need bridge?            → ADAPTER" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1420" width="1520" height="480" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1940" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: Real examples of Facade Pattern in Java libraries?&#xa;A1: (1) java.net.URL — hides HTTP protocol complexity (TCP sockets, DNS, headers). Just call openStream(). (2) JDBC DriverManager — hides driver loading, connection pooling. Just getConnection(url). (3) SLF4J logging — hides Logback/Log4j complexity. Just logger.info(). (4) Spring Framework — ApplicationContext hides bean creation, DI, lifecycle. Just getBean(). Everywhere you see 'simple API hiding complex subsystem' = Facade!&#xa;&#xa;Q2: When NOT to use Facade Pattern?&#xa;A2: Don't use when: (1) Subsystem is already simple (over-engineering), (2) Client needs fine-grained control (facade limits flexibility), (3) Performance critical (extra layer adds latency), (4) Subsystem changes frequently (facade becomes bottleneck). Use when subsystem is COMPLEX and STABLE, client wants SIMPLICITY.&#xa;&#xa;Q3: Can Facade use another Facade?&#xa;A3: YES! Layered facades. Example: CheckoutFacade uses OrderFacade + PaymentFacade. OrderFacade orchestrates ProductDao, Invoice. PaymentFacade orchestrates PaymentGateway, FraudDetection. Each facade simplifies its layer. Client calls CheckoutFacade.checkout() — doesn't know about nested facades. Powerful composition!&#xa;&#xa;Q4: Facade Pattern violates Open-Closed Principle?&#xa;A4: Can violate if not designed well. PROBLEM: Adding new subsystem operation → must modify facade (closed for modification violated). SOLUTION: (1) Create multiple small facades (OrderFacade, PaymentFacade) instead of one god facade, (2) Use Strategy/Template Method inside facade for extensibility, (3) Accept that facade IS the coordination point — some modification expected. Trade-off: simplicity vs strict OCP.&#xa;&#xa;Q5: Facade vs Service Layer in Spring — same thing?&#xa;A5: Similar concept! Service Layer (@Service) is facade over repositories (@Repository). Example: OrderService (facade) orchestrates ProductRepository, PaymentRepository, NotificationService. Controller calls OrderService.createOrder() — doesn't know repository details. Service Layer = Facade Pattern in layered architecture. Both hide complexity, provide simplified interface!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1990" width="1520" height="460" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
